DOM = Document Object Model.

When the browser loads HTML:
<h1>Hello Arun</h1>
<button>Click Me</button>

The browser creates a DOM tree:
HTML
 └── body
      ├── h1
      │    └── Hello Arun
      │
      └── button
           └── Click Me

If something changes, the browser may need to update the real DOM.

Virtual DOM - React creates a Virtual DOM, which is a lightweight JavaScript representation of the UI.

Example :-
function App() {
  return <h1>Hello Arun</h1>;
}

React internally represents this UI approximately like :-
Virtual DOM
     |
     └── h1
          |
          └── Hello Arun

Now suppose we change :-
<h1>Hello Arun</h1>
to :-
<h1>Hello Theja</h1>

React compares :-
Old Virtual DOM → <h1>Hello Arun</h1>
New Virtual DOM → <h1>Hello Theja</h1>

React identifies what changed :-
Hello Arun → Hello Theja

Then React updates the required part of the real DOM.

Flow :-
State/Data changes
       ↓
React creates new Virtual DOM
       ↓
Compares with old Virtual DOM
       ↓
Finds what changed
       ↓
Updates required Real DOM
       ↓
Browser displays updated UI
